  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a complex, multi-tiered relationship between Npc147835 and Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH), with Npc147835 acting as a target for several compounds that ultimately inhibit GAPDH. These compounds, including 6-Bromo-8-Methoxy-4-Oxo-1H-Quinoline-2-Carboxylic Acid and its structural analogs, are all known to target the Prostaglandin D2 receptor, which in turn is a target of Npc147835. This indicates that Npc147835 may be a modulator of the Prostaglandin D2 receptor, potentially influencing downstream pathways involving GAPDH. The presence of multiple distinct compounds targeting the same receptor suggests that Npc147835 may function as a bridge between the receptor and GAPDH, though the exact mechanism of this interaction remains unclear. The evidence supports a plausible mechanistic link, but further investigation is needed to confirm the direct role of Npc147835 in GAPDH inhibition.",
